    "solution": "Let the dimensions of the fundamental constants be:\n1. Universal Gravitational Constant (G): From F = Gm₁m₂/r², dimensions of G = F * r² / (m₁m₂) = [MLT⁻²][L²]/[M²] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]\n2. Speed of Light (c): Dimensions of c = [LT⁻¹]\n3. Planck's Constant (h): From E = hf, dimensions of h = E/f = [ML²T⁻²]/[T⁻¹] = [ML²T⁻¹]\n\nWe are looking for a combination Gˣcʸhᶻ that has the dimensions of length [L].\nSo, [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]ˣ * [LT⁻¹]ʸ * [ML²T⁻¹]ᶻ = [L¹M⁰T⁰]\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T:\n\nFor M: -x + z = 0  => x = z  --- (1)\n\nFor L: 3x + y + 2z = 1 --- (2)\n\nFor T: -2x - y - z = 0 --- (3)\n\nSubstitute (1) into (3):\n-2x - y - x = 0\n-3x - y = 0  => y = -3x --- (4)\n\nNow substitute (1) and (4) into (2):\n3x + (-3x) + 2x = 1\n2x = 1\nx = 1/2\n\nFrom (1), z = x = 1/2\nFrom (4), y = -3x = -3(1/2) = -3/2\n\nSo, the combination is G^(1/2) * c^(-3/2) * h^(1/2).\nThis can be written as √(G * h / c³).\n\nTherefore, the combination √(Gh/c³) has the dimensions of length. This is known as the Planck length.",
