    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Let the dimension of length [L] in this new system be expressed as a combination of c, G, and h with some powers:\n[L] = c^x G^y h^z\n\nFirst, let's write down the standard dimensions of c, G, and h:\n1.  Speed of light (c): [L T⁻¹]\n2.  Universal gravitational constant (G): From Newton's law of gravitation, F = GMm/r².\n    So, [G] = [F r² / Mm] = [M L T⁻² L² / M²] = [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]\n3.  Planck's constant (h): From the energy of a photon, E = hν (where ν is frequency).\n    So, [h] = [E / ν] = [M L² T⁻² / T⁻¹] = [M L² T⁻¹]\n\nNow, substitute these dimensions into the expression for [L]:\n[L¹ M⁰ T⁰] = ([L T⁻¹])^x ([M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²])^y ([M L² T⁻¹])^z\n[L¹ M⁰ T⁰] = [L^(x+3y+2z) M^(-y+z) T^(-x-2y-z)]\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T on both sides:\n\nFor M: 0 = -y + z  =>  y = z  (Equation 1)\n\nFor T: 0 = -x - 2y - z  (Equation 2)\n\nFor L: 1 = x + 3y + 2z  (Equation 3)\n\nSubstitute (Equation 1) into (Equation 2):\n0 = -x - 2y - y\n0 = -x - 3y  =>  x = -3y  (Equation 4)\n\nNow substitute (Equation 1) (y=z) and (Equation 4) (x=-3y) into (Equation 3):\n1 = (-3y) + 3y + 2y\n1 = 2y\ny = 1/2\n\nNow, find z and x:\nFrom (Equation 1): z = y = 1/2\nFrom (Equation 4): x = -3y = -3(1/2) = -3/2\n\nSo, the dimension of length [L] is:\n[L] = c^(-3/2) G^(1/2) h^(1/2)\n[L] = (G^(1/2) h^(1/2)) / c^(3/2)\n[L] = √(Gh / c³)\n\nThus, the correct option is A.",
