    "solution": "Let the dimension of mass [M] be expressed in terms of the new fundamental units as:\n[M] = [F]^x [V]^y [T]^z\n\nFirst, write down the dimensions of F, V, and T in terms of standard fundamental units (Mass M, Length L, Time T):\n[F] = MLT⁻²\n[V] = LT⁻¹\n[T] = T\n\nSubstitute these into the expression for [M]:\nM = (MLT⁻²)^x (LT⁻¹)^y (T)^z\nM = M^x L^x T^(-2x) L^y T^(-y) T^z\n\nGroup the powers of M, L, and T:\nM¹L⁰T⁰ = M^x L^(x+y) T^(-2x-y+z)\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T on both sides:\nFor M: x = 1 (Equation 1)\nFor L: x + y = 0 (Equation 2)\nFor T: -2x - y + z = 0 (Equation 3)\n\nFrom Equation 1, x = 1.\nSubstitute x = 1 into Equation 2:\n1 + y = 0\ny = -1\n\nSubstitute x = 1 and y = -1 into Equation 3:\n-2(1) - (-1) + z = 0\n-2 + 1 + z = 0\n-1 + z = 0\nz = 1\n\nSo, the dimensions of mass in this new system are:\n[M] = [F]¹ [V]⁻¹ [T]¹ = [F][V]⁻¹[T]",
