    "question": "In a hypothetical system of units, if the unit of length is increased by 10%, the unit of mass is increased by 20%, and the unit of time is increased by 30%, what is the approximate percentage change in the unit of force?",
    "option_a": "15.5% decrease",
    "option_b": "21.9% decrease",
    "option_c": "18.3% increase",
    "option_d": "25.0% increase",
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "solution": "The dimension of force [F] is MLT\u207b\u00b2.\n\nLet the original units of length, mass, and time be L, M, and T, respectively. The original unit of force is F = MLT\u207b\u00b2.\n\nAccording to the problem:\nNew unit of length L' = L + 0.10L = 1.1L\nNew unit of mass M' = M + 0.20M = 1.2M\nNew unit of time T' = T + 0.30T = 1.3T\n\nThe new unit of force F' in terms of the new units is:\nF' = M'L'(T')\u207b\u00b2\nF' = (1.2M)(1.1L)(1.3T)\u207b\u00b2\nF' = (1.2)(1.1)(1.3)\u207b\u00b2 MLT\u207b\u00b2\nF' = (1.32) / (1.69) MLT\u207b\u00b2\nF' \u2248 0.781 MLT\u207b\u00b2\n\nSo, F' \u2248 0.781 F.\n\nTo find the percentage change in the unit of force:\nPercentage Change = ((F' - F) / F) \u00d7 100%\nPercentage Change = ((0.781F - F) / F) \u00d7 100%\nPercentage Change = (-0.219F / F) \u00d7 100%\nPercentage Change = -0.219 \u00d7 100% = -21.9%\n\nThis means the unit of force decreases by approximately 21.9%.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Unit Conversion and Scaling"
  },
  {
    "question": "The Poynting vector S, which describes the energy flux of an electromagnetic field, is defined as S = (1/\u03bc\u2080)(E \u00d7 B), where \u03bc\u2080 is the permeability of free space, E is the electric field, and B is the magnetic field. What are the dimensions of S?",
    "option_a": "ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b3",
    "option_b": "ML\u207b\u00b9T\u207b\u00b3",
    "option_c": "MT\u207b\u00b3",
    "option_d": "M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b3",
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "The Poynting vector S represents power per unit area (energy flux).\nIts dimension should be [Power]/[Area].\n[Power] = [Work/Time] = [ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2/T] = ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b3\n[Area] = L\u00b2\nSo, [S] = [ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b3]/[L\u00b2] = MT\u207b\u00b3.\n\nLet's verify this using the given formula S = (1/\u03bc\u2080)(E \u00d7 B):\n1.  Dimension of Electric Field [E]:\n    [E] = [Force/Charge] = [MLT\u207b\u00b2/AT] = MLT\u207b\u00b3A\u207b\u00b9\n\n2.  Dimension of Magnetic Field [B]:\n    [B] = [Force/(Charge \u00d7 Velocity)] = [MLT\u207b\u00b2/(AT \u00d7 LT\u207b\u00b9)] = [MLT\u207b\u00b2/(AL)] = MT\u207b\u00b2A\u207b\u00b9\n\n3.  Dimension of Permeability of Free Space [\u03bc\u2080]:\n    We know the speed of light c = 1/\u221a(\u03bc\u2080\u03b5\u2080), so \u03bc\u2080 = 1/(c\u00b2\u03b5\u2080).\n    [c] = LT\u207b\u00b9\n    [\u03b5\u2080] = [M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b3T\u2074A\u00b2] (from Coulomb's law F = 1/(4\u03c0\u03b5\u2080) q1q2/r\u00b2)\n    [\u03bc\u2080] = 1/[(LT\u207b\u00b9)\u00b2(M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b3T\u2074A\u00b2)] = 1/[L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b3T\u2074A\u00b2] = 1/[M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b9T\u00b2A\u00b2] = MLT\u207b\u00b2A\u207b\u00b2\n\n4.  Now, calculate the dimension of S = (1/\u03bc\u2080)(E \u00d7 B):\n    [1/\u03bc\u2080] = M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b9T\u00b2A\u00b2\n    [E \u00d7 B] = [E][B] = (MLT\u207b\u00b3A\u207b\u00b9)(MT\u207b\u00b2A\u207b\u00b9) = M\u00b2LT\u207b\u2075A\u207b\u00b2\n\n    [S] = [1/\u03bc\u2080][E \u00d7 B] = (M\u207b\u00b9L\u207b\u00b9T\u00b2A\u00b2)(M\u00b2LT\u207b\u2075A\u207b\u00b2)\n    [S] = M^(-1+2) L^(-1+1) T^(2-5) A^(2-2)\n    [S] = M\u00b9L\u2070T\u207b\u00b3A\u2070 = MT\u207b\u00b3\n\nBoth methods yield the same dimension, MT\u207b\u00b3.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensions of Electromagnetic Quantities"
  },
  {
    "question": "Consider a hypothetical system where force (F), velocity (V), and time (T) are chosen as fundamental physical quantities. What are the dimensions of mass in this new system?",
    "option_a": "[F][V]\u207b\u00b9[T]",
    "option_b": "[F]\u207b\u00b9[V][T]",
    "option_c": "[F][V][T]\u207b\u00b9",
    "option_d": "[F]\u207b\u00b9[V]\u207b\u00b9[T]",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Let the dimension of mass [M] be expressed in terms of the new fundamental units as:\n[M] = [F]^x [V]^y [T]^z\n\nFirst, write down the dimensions of F, V, and T in terms of standard fundamental units (Mass M, Length L, Time T):\n[F] = MLT\u207b\u00b2\n[V] = LT\u207b\u00b9\n[T] = T\n\nSubstitute these into the expression for [M]:\nM = (MLT\u207b\u00b2)^x (LT\u207b\u00b9)^y (T)^z\nM = M^x L^x T^(-2x) L^y T^(-y) T^z\n\nGroup the powers of M, L, and T:\nM\u00b9L\u2070T\u2070 = M^x L^(x+y) T^(-2x-y+z)\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T on both sides:\nFor M: x = 1 (Equation 1)\nFor L: x + y = 0 (Equation 2)\nFor T: -2x - y + z = 0 (Equation 3)\n\nFrom Equation 1, x = 1.\nSubstitute x = 1 into Equation 2:\n1 + y = 0\ny = -1\n\nSubstitute x = 1 and y = -1 into Equation 3:\n-2(1) - (-1) + z = 0\n-2 + 1 + z = 0\n-1 + z = 0\nz = 1\n\nSo, the dimensions of mass in this new system are:\n[M] = [F]\u00b9 [V]\u207b\u00b9 [T]\u00b9 = [F][V]\u207b\u00b9[T]",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional Analysis using Derived Fundamental Units"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following combinations of universal fundamental constants has the dimension of time?",
    "option_a": "\u221a(hG/c\u00b3)",
    "option_b": "\u221a(hG/c\u2075)",
    "option_c": "\u221a(Gc/h)",
    "option_d": "\u221a(h/Gc\u00b3)",
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "solution": "Let's find the dimensions of each option using the dimensions of Planck's constant (h), gravitational constant (G), and speed of light (c):\n[h] = ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9 (Dimension of angular momentum or energy \u00d7 time)\n[G] = M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b2 (From F = GMm/r\u00b2)\n[c] = LT\u207b\u00b9 (Dimension of velocity)\n\nLet's analyze each option:\n\nOption A: \u221a(hG/c\u00b3)\n[hG] = (ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9)(M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b2) = M\u2070L\u2075T\u207b\u00b3\n[c\u00b3] = (LT\u207b\u00b9)\u00b3 = L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b3\n[hG/c\u00b3] = [L\u2075T\u207b\u00b3] / [L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b3] = L\u00b2\n\u221a(hG/c\u00b3) = \u221a(L\u00b2) = L (This is Planck Length)\n\nOption B: \u221a(hG/c\u2075)\n[hG] = M\u2070L\u2075T\u207b\u00b3 (as calculated above)\n[c\u2075] = (LT\u207b\u00b9)\u2075 = L\u2075T\u207b\u2075\n[hG/c\u2075] = [L\u2075T\u207b\u00b3] / [L\u2075T\u207b\u2075] = T^(\u207b\u00b3 - (\u207b\u2075)) = T\u00b2\n\u221a(hG/c\u2075) = \u221a(T\u00b2) = T (This is Planck Time)\n\nOption C: \u221a(Gc/h)\n[Gc] = (M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b2)(LT\u207b\u00b9) = M\u207b\u00b9L\u2074T\u207b\u00b3\n[Gc/h] = [M\u207b\u00b9L\u2074T\u207b\u00b3] / [ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9] = M^(-1-1) L^(4-2) T^(-3-(-1)) = M\u207b\u00b2L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2\n\u221a(Gc/h) = \u221a(M\u207b\u00b2L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2) = M\u207b\u00b9LT\u207b\u00b9 (This is dimension of 1/v or 1/(mc)) - not time.\n\nOption D: \u221a(h/Gc\u00b3)\n[h] = ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9\n[Gc\u00b3] = [M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b2] \u00d7 [L\u00b3T\u207b\u00b3] = M\u207b\u00b9L\u2076T\u207b\u2075\n[h/Gc\u00b3] = [ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9] / [M\u207b\u00b9L\u2076T\u207b\u2075] = M^(1-(-1)) L^(2-6) T^(-1-(-5)) = M\u00b2L\u207b\u2074T\u2074\n\u221a(h/Gc\u00b3) = \u221a(M\u00b2L\u207b\u2074T\u2074) = ML\u207b\u00b2T\u00b2 - not time.\n\nTherefore, the combination \u221a(hG/c\u2075) has the dimension of time.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional Analysis of Fundamental Constants"
  },
  {
    "question": "If Planck's constant (h) is expressed in terms of Energy (E), Length (L), and Time (T) as fundamental units, what would be its dimension?",
    "option_a": "[E][T]",
    "option_b": "[E][L]\u207b\u00b9[T]",
    "option_c": "[E][L][T]\u207b\u00b9",
    "option_d": "[E]\u207b\u00b9[L][T]",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Let the dimension of Planck's constant [h] be expressed in terms of the new fundamental units as:\n[h] = [E]^x [L]^y [T]^z\n\nFirst, write down the dimensions of h, E, L, and T in terms of standard fundamental units (Mass M, Length L, Time T):\n[h] = ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9 (from E = hf, so h = E/f = E*T)\n[E] = ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2 (from E = Work = Force \u00d7 Length)\n[L] = L\n[T] = T\n\nSubstitute these into the expression for [h]:\nML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9 = (ML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b2)^x (L)^y (T)^z\nML\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9 = M^x L^(2x) T^(-2x) L^y T^z\n\nGroup the powers of M, L, and T:\nM\u00b9L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9 = M^x L^(2x+y) T^(-2x+z)\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T on both sides:\nFor M: x = 1 (Equation 1)\nFor L: 2x + y = 2 (Equation 2)\nFor T: -2x + z = -1 (Equation 3)\n\nFrom Equation 1, x = 1.\n\nSubstitute x = 1 into Equation 2:\n2(1) + y = 2\n2 + y = 2\ny = 0\n\nSubstitute x = 1 into Equation 3:\n-2(1) + z = -1\n-2 + z = -1\nz = 1\n\nSo, the dimensions of Planck's constant in this new system are:\n[h] = [E]\u00b9 [L]\u2070 [T]\u00b9 = [E][T]",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional Analysis using Derived Fundamental Units"
  },
  {
    "question": "A physical quantity X is defined as X = (\u03b7L\u00b2) / (\u03c1V), where \u03b7 is the coefficient of viscosity, L is length, \u03c1 is density, and V is velocity. What are the dimensions of X?",
    "option_a": "L\u00b2",
    "option_b": "L\u00b3",
    "option_c": "LT",
    "option_d": "M\u207b\u00b9L\u00b3T",
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "solution": "First, identify the dimensions of each quantity:\n1.  Coefficient of viscosity (\u03b7):\n    From Newton's law of viscosity, F = \u03b7A(dv/dy), where F is force, A is area, dv/dy is velocity gradient.\n    [\u03b7] = [F] / ([A][dv/dy]) = [MLT\u207b\u00b2] / ([L\u00b2][(LT\u207b\u00b9)/L]) = [MLT\u207b\u00b2] / [L\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9] = ML\u207b\u00b9T\u207b\u00b9\n\n2.  Length (L): [L] = L\n\n3.  Density (\u03c1): [\u03c1] = [Mass/Volume] = ML\u207b\u00b3\n\n4.  Velocity (V): [V] = LT\u207b\u00b9\n\nNow, substitute these dimensions into the expression for X:\nX = (\u03b7L\u00b2) / (\u03c1V)\n[X] = ([ML\u207b\u00b9T\u207b\u00b9][L\u00b2]) / ([ML\u207b\u00b3][LT\u207b\u00b9])\n\nSimplify the numerator:\n[Numerator] = ML\u207b\u00b9T\u207b\u00b9L\u00b2 = ML\u00b9T\u207b\u00b9\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n[Denominator] = ML\u207b\u00b3LT\u207b\u00b9 = ML\u207b\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9\n\nNow, divide the numerator by the denominator:\n[X] = [ML\u00b9T\u207b\u00b9] / [ML\u207b\u00b2T\u207b\u00b9]\n[X] = M^(1-1) L^(1-(-2)) T^(-1-(-1))\n[X] = M\u2070L\u00b3T\u2070 = L\u00b3\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of X are L\u00b3.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional Analysis of Complex Expressions"
  }
