    "solution": "The formula for the period of a simple pendulum is T = 2\u03c0\u221a(L/g).\nWe need to find the percentage error in g. Rearranging the formula for g:\nT^2 = 4\u03c0^2 L/g\ng = 4\u03c0^2 L/T^2\n\nTo find the maximum percentage error in g, we use the formula for error propagation for products and quotients:\n\u0394g/g = \u0394L/L + 2(\u0394T/T)\n\nGiven values and their uncertainties:\nLength L = 20.0 cm\nAccuracy in L = 1 mm = 0.1 cm\nSo, \u0394L = 0.1 cm\nRelative error in L: \u0394L/L = 0.1/20.0 = 1/200\n\nTime for 100 oscillations = 90 s\nResolution of wrist watch = 1 s\nSo, \u0394t = 1 s (error in total time measured)\n\nThe period T is calculated from the total time 't' for 'N' oscillations: T = t/N.\nHere, N = 100 (assumed to be exact count).\nSo, T = 90 s / 100 = 0.9 s\n\nThe relative error in T is \u0394T/T = \u0394t/t.\nRelative error in T: \u0394T/T = 1/90\n\nNow, substitute these relative errors into the formula for \u0394g/g:\n\u0394g/g = (1/200) + 2 * (1/90)\n\u0394g/g = 1/200 + 1/45\n\nTo add these fractions, find a common denominator, which is LCM(200, 45) = 1800.\n\u0394g/g = (9/1800) + (40/1800)\n\u0394g/g = 49/1800\n\nPercentage error in g = (\u0394g/g) * 100%\nPercentage error = (49/1800) * 100% = 49/18 %\nPercentage error \u2248 2.722...\n\nRounding to one decimal place, the maximum percentage error is 2.7%.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Error analysis and propagation"
  },
  {
    "question": "In a hypothetical system of units, the unit of force (F_new) is 10 N, the unit of energy (E_new) is 200 J, and the unit of velocity (V_new) is 5 m/s. What will be the unit of length in this new system?",
    "option_a": "20 m",
    "option_b": "10 m",
    "option_c": "4 m",
    "option_d": "40 m",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Let the fundamental units in the new system be M_new, L_new, T_new. We are given their derived values:\nUnit of Force (F_new): [M L T^-2] = 10 N\nUnit of Energy (E_new): [M L^2 T^-2] = 200 J\nUnit of Velocity (V_new): [L T^-1] = 5 m/s\n\nWe need to find the unit of length (L_new).\n\nLet's express the dimensions of force, energy, and velocity in terms of M, L, T:\n1. F_new = M_new L_new T_new^-2 = 10 kg m s^-2\n2. E_new = M_new L_new^2 T_new^-2 = 200 kg m^2 s^-2\n3. V_new = L_new T_new^-1 = 5 m s^-1\n\nDivide the equation for Energy by the equation for Force:\n(E_new) / (F_new) = (M_new L_new^2 T_new^-2) / (M_new L_new T_new^-2)\nE_new / F_new = L_new\n\nNow, substitute the given numerical values:\nL_new = 200 J / 10 N\nL_new = 200 (kg m^2 s^-2) / 10 (kg m s^-2)\nL_new = 20 m\n\nThus, the unit of length in this new system is 20 meters.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Unit conversion and new systems of units"
  },
  {
    "question": "An equation for the velocity (v) of a wave in a fluid is proposed as v = k * (P/\u03c1)^m * (\u03b7/\u03c1)^n, where k is a dimensionless constant, P is pressure, \u03c1 is density, and \u03b7 is the coefficient of viscosity. Find the values of m and n.",
    "option_a": "m = 1/2, n = 0",
    "option_b": "m = 0, n = 1/2",
    "option_c": "m = 1, n = -1",
    "option_d": "m = 1/2, n = 1/2",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "We will use the principle of dimensional homogeneity. The dimensions of the left side must be equal to the dimensions of the right side.\n\nDimensions of the quantities involved:\nv (velocity): [L T^-1]\nP (pressure): [M L^-1 T^-2] (Force/Area)\n\u03c1 (density): [M L^-3] (Mass/Volume)\n\u03b7 (coefficient of viscosity): [M L^-1 T^-1] (from F = \u03b7A(dv/dr), F=[MLT^-2], A=[L^2], dv/dr=[T^-1])\nk (dimensionless constant): [M^0 L^0 T^0]\n\nThe given equation is v = k * (P/\u03c1)^m * (\u03b7/\u03c1)^n.\n\nLet's find the dimensions of (P/\u03c1) and (\u03b7/\u03c1):\nDimensions of (P/\u03c1): ([M L^-1 T^-2]) / ([M L^-3]) = [L^2 T^-2]\nDimensions of (\u03b7/\u03c1): ([M L^-1 T^-1]) / ([M L^-3]) = [L^2 T^-1]\n\nNow, substitute these into the equation:\n[L T^-1] = ([L^2 T^-2])^m * ([L^2 T^-1])^n\n[L T^-1] = [L^(2m) T^(-2m)] * [L^(2n) T^(-n)]\n[L T^-1] = [L^(2m+2n) T^(-2m-n)]\n\nEquating the powers of L and T on both sides:\nFor L: 1 = 2m + 2n  (Equation 1)\nFor T: -1 = -2m - n => 1 = 2m + n (Equation 2)\n\nNow, we solve the system of linear equations:\nSubtract Equation 2 from Equation 1:\n(2m + 2n) - (2m + n) = 1 - 1\nn = 0\n\nSubstitute n = 0 into Equation 2:\n1 = 2m + 0\n2m = 1 => m = 1/2\n\nThus, the values are m = 1/2 and n = 0.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional analysis of empirical relations"
  },
  {
    "question": "If Force (F), Velocity (V), and Energy (E) are chosen as fundamental quantities, then the dimensional formula for Mass (M) in terms of these new fundamental quantities would be:",
    "option_a": "[E V^-2]",
    "option_b": "[F V E^-1]",
    "option_c": "[F E V^-3]",
    "option_d": "[E F^-1 V^-1]",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "The standard dimensions of the given quantities are:\nForce (F): [M L T^-2]\nVelocity (V): [L T^-1]\nEnergy (E): [M L^2 T^-2]\n\nWe want to express Mass (M) in terms of F, V, and E. Let M = F^x V^y E^z.\n[M] = ([M L T^-2])^x ([L T^-1])^y ([M L^2 T^-2])^z\n[M] = [M^(x+z) L^(x+y+2z) T^(-2x-y-2z)]\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, T on both sides:\nFor M: 1 = x + z     (Equation 1)\nFor L: 0 = x + y + 2z  (Equation 2)\nFor T: 0 = -2x - y - 2z (Equation 3)\n\nFrom Equation 1, x = 1 - z.\n\nSubstitute x into Equation 3:\n0 = -2(1 - z) - y - 2z\n0 = -2 + 2z - y - 2z\n0 = -2 - y => y = -2\n\nSubstitute x = 1 - z and y = -2 into Equation 2:\n0 = (1 - z) + (-2) + 2z\n0 = 1 - z - 2 + 2z\n0 = -1 + z => z = 1\n\nNow find x using z = 1:\nx = 1 - z = 1 - 1 = 0\n\nSo, the powers are x = 0, y = -2, z = 1.\nTherefore, M = F^0 V^-2 E^1 = [E V^-2].",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Derived units from new fundamental quantities"
