  {
    "question": "If the speed of light (c), the universal gravitational constant (G), and Planck's constant (h) are chosen as fundamental units, then the dimension of time in terms of these new fundamental units would be:",
    "option_a": "c^(1/2) G^(1/2) h^(-3/2)",
    "option_b": "c^(-5/2) G^(1/2) h^(1/2)",
    "option_c": "c^(3/2) G^(-1/2) h^(1/2)",
    "option_d": "c^(-1/2) G^(1/2) h^(-1/2)",
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "solution": "The dimensions of the given constants are:\nSpeed of light c: [L T^-1]\nGravitational constant G: [M^-1 L^3 T^-2]\nPlanck's constant h: [M L^2 T^-1]\n\nWe want to find the dimension of Time, [T], in terms of c, G, h. Let [T] = c^x G^y h^z.\n[T] = ([L T^-1])^x ([M^-1 L^3 T^-2])^y ([M L^2 T^-1])^z\n[T] = [M^(-y+z) L^(x+3y+2z) T^(-x-2y-z)]\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, T:\nFor M: 0 = -y + z  => y = z (Equation 1)\nFor L: 0 = x + 3y + 2z (Equation 2)\nFor T: 1 = -x - 2y - z (Equation 3)\n\nSubstitute (1) into (2) and (3):\nFrom (2): 0 = x + 3y + 2y => x + 5y = 0 => x = -5y (Equation 4)\nFrom (3): 1 = -x - 2y - y => 1 = -x - 3y (Equation 5)\n\nSubstitute (4) into (5):\n1 = -(-5y) - 3y\n1 = 5y - 3y\n1 = 2y => y = 1/2\n\nFrom (1), z = y = 1/2\nFrom (4), x = -5y = -5(1/2) = -5/2\n\nTherefore, x = -5/2, y = 1/2, z = 1/2.\nThe dimension of time is c^(-5/2) G^(1/2) h^(1/2). This combination is known as Planck time.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional analysis of fundamental constants"
  },
  {
    "question": "In the equation P = (a - bt^2)/(Vx), where P is pressure, t is time, V is volume, and x is distance, find the dimensions of constants 'a' and 'b'.",
    "option_a": "a: [M L^3 T^-2], b: [M L^3 T^-4]",
    "option_b": "a: [M L^-1 T^-2], b: [M L^-1 T^0]",
