    "option_d": "a: [M L^5 T^-2], b: [M L^5 T^-4]",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "According to the principle of dimensional homogeneity, each term in an addition or subtraction must have the same dimensions. Also, the dimensions on both sides of an equation must be identical.\n\nGiven equation: P = (a - bt^2)/(Vx)\n\nDimensions of known quantities:\nP (Pressure): [M L^-1 T^-2]\nt (time): [T]\nV (Volume): [L^3]\nx (distance): [L]\n\n1. Dimensions of the denominator (Vx):\n[Vx] = [L^3] * [L] = [L^4]\n\n2. Dimensions of the numerator (a - bt^2):\nFor the equation to be dimensionally consistent, the dimensions of the numerator (a - bt^2) must be equal to the dimensions of (P * Vx).\nDimensions of (P * Vx) = [M L^-1 T^-2] * [L^4] = [M L^3 T^-2]\n\nSince (a - bt^2) must have the dimensions of [M L^3 T^-2], it implies:\n[a] = [M L^3 T^-2]\n\nAnd, for the subtraction to be valid, [bt^2] must also have the same dimensions as [a]:\n[bt^2] = [M L^3 T^-2]\n\nNow, find the dimensions of 'b':\n[b] * [T^2] = [M L^3 T^-2]\n[b] = [M L^3 T^-2] / [T^2]\n[b] = [M L^3 T^-4]\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of 'a' are [M L^3 T^-2] and 'b' are [M L^3 T^-4].",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "topic": "Dimensional homogeneity and constants"
  },
  {
    "question": "A wire has a length L = 100 \u00b1 0.1 cm, radius r = 0.50 \u00b1 0.005 cm, and mass M = 50.0 \u00b1 0.05 g. The maximum percentage error in the determination of its density (\u03c1) is:",
    "option_a": "1.8%",
