    "option_d": "2.5%",
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "The density (\u03c1) of the wire is given by the formula \u03c1 = M/V, where V is the volume of the wire.\nAssuming the wire is cylindrical, its volume V = \u03c0r^2L.\nSo, the formula for density is \u03c1 = M / (\u03c0r^2L).\n\nTo find the maximum percentage error in \u03c1, we use the error propagation formula for products and quotients:\n\u0394\u03c1/\u03c1 = \u0394M/M + 2(\u0394r/r) + \u0394L/L (since \u03c0 is a constant, its error is zero)\n\nGiven uncertainties:\nMass M = 50.0 g, \u0394M = 0.05 g\nRelative error in mass: \u0394M/M = 0.05 / 50.0 = 1/1000\n\nRadius r = 0.50 cm, \u0394r = 0.005 cm\nRelative error in radius: \u0394r/r = 0.005 / 0.50 = 5/500 = 1/100\n\nLength L = 100 cm, \u0394L = 0.1 cm\nRelative error in length: \u0394L/L = 0.1 / 100 = 1/1000\n\nNow, substitute these relative errors into the formula for \u0394\u03c1/\u03c1:\n\u0394\u03c1/\u03c1 = (1/1000) + 2 * (1/100) + (1/1000)\n\u0394\u03c1/\u03c1 = 1/1000 + 20/1000 + 1/1000\n\u0394\u03c1/\u03c1 = (1 + 20 + 1) / 1000\n\u0394\u03c1/\u03c1 = 22/1000\n\nTo find the maximum percentage error, multiply by 100%:\nPercentage error = (22/1000) * 100% = 22/10 % = 2.2%\n\nThus, the maximum percentage error in the determination of the density is 2.2%.",
