    "solution": "Let the dimensional formula for mass in terms of G, h, and c be:\n[Mass] = [G]^a [h]^b [c]^k\n\nFirst, we write down the conventional dimensions of G, h, and c:\n1.  Gravitational Constant (G):\n    From Newton's Law of Gravitation, F = G(m1m2)/r^2. So, G = Fr^2/(m1m2).\n    [G] = [MLT^-2][L^2]/[M^2] = [M^-1 L^3 T^-2]\n\n2.  Planck's Constant (h):\n    From E = hν (where E is energy and ν is frequency), h = E/ν.\n    [h] = [ML^2T^-2]/[T^-1] = [ML^2T^-1]\n    (Alternatively, from angular momentum L = Iω = h/2π, [h] = [ML^2T^-1])\n\n3.  Speed of light (c):\n    [c] = [LT^-1]\n\nNow substitute these into our assumed relationship for mass:\nM^1 L^0 T^0 = ([M^-1 L^3 T^-2])^a ([M L^2 T^-1])^b ([L T^-1])^k\nM^1 L^0 T^0 = M^(-a+b) L^(3a+2b+k) T^(-2a-b-k)\n\nEquating the powers of M, L, and T on both sides:\n\nFor M:  -a + b = 1  (Equation 1)\nFor L:   3a + 2b + k = 0 (Equation 2)\nFor T:  -2a - b - k = 0 (Equation 3)\n\nAdd Equation 2 and Equation 3:\n(3a + 2b + k) + (-2a - b - k) = 0 + 0\na + b = 0  (Equation 4)\n\nNow we have a system of two simpler equations for 'a' and 'b':\n1.  -a + b = 1\n4.   a + b = 0\n\nAdd Equation 1 and Equation 4:\n(-a + b) + (a + b) = 1 + 0\n2b = 1\nb = 1/2\n\nSubstitute 'b' back into Equation 4:\na + (1/2) = 0\na = -1/2\n\nFinally, substitute 'a' and 'b' into Equation 3 to find 'k':\n-2(-1/2) - (1/2) - k = 0\n1 - 1/2 - k = 0\n1/2 - k = 0\nk = 1/2\n\nSo, the values are a = -1/2, b = 1/2, and k = 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the dimensional formula for mass is:\n[Mass] = [G]^(-1/2) [h]^(1/2) [c]^(1/2)",
