    "question": "Which of the following molecules has a square planar geometry?",
    "option_a": "SF4",
    "option_b": "XeF4",
    "option_c": "PCl5",
    "option_d": "NH3",
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "solution": "To determine the geometry, we use VSEPR theory. For XeF4:\n1.  Central atom: Xe (Group 18), valence electrons = 8.\n2.  Bonded atoms: 4 F atoms. Each F forms a single bond.\n3.  Electron pairs used for bonding = 4.\n4.  Lone pairs = (8 - 4)/2 = 2.\n5.  Total electron domains (steric number) = 4 (bond pairs) + 2 (lone pairs) = 6.\n6.  A steric number of 6 corresponds to sp3d2 hybridization, which has an octahedral electron geometry.\n7.  With 4 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs, the lone pairs occupy positions to minimize repulsion, leading to a square planar molecular geometry. The lone pairs are trans to each other, pushing the four F atoms into a plane.\n\nLet's check other options:\nSF4: 4 bond pairs, 1 lone pair (steric number 5, sp3d hybridization) -> seesaw geometry.\nPCl5: 5 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs (steric number 5, sp3d hybridization) -> trigonal bipyramidal geometry.\nNH3: 3 bond pairs, 1 lone pair (steric number 4, sp3 hybridization) -> trigonal pyramidal geometry.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
