    "solution": "Bond order is calculated using the Molecular Orbital Theory (MOT) formula: Bond Order = 1/2 (Nb - Na), where Nb is the number of electrons in bonding molecular orbitals and Na is the number of electrons in antibonding molecular orbitals.\n\n1.  O2: Total electrons = 8 + 8 = 16.\n    Electron configuration: (σ1s)^2 (σ*1s)^2 (σ2s)^2 (σ*2s)^2 (σ2pz)^2 (π2px)^2 (π2py)^2 (π*2px)^1 (π*2py)^1.\n    Nb = 2+2+2+2+2 = 10, Na = 2+2+1+1 = 6.\n    Bond Order = 1/2 (10 - 6) = 2.\n\n2.  O2+: Total electrons = 16 - 1 = 15.\n    Electron configuration: (σ1s)^2 (σ*1s)^2 (σ2s)^2 (σ*2s)^2 (σ2pz)^2 (π2px)^2 (π2py)^2 (π*2px)^1.\n    Nb = 10, Na = 5.\n    Bond Order = 1/2 (10 - 5) = 2.5.\n\n3.  O2-: Total electrons = 16 + 1 = 17.\n    Electron configuration: (σ1s)^2 (σ*1s)^2 (σ2s)^2 (σ*2s)^2 (σ2pz)^2 (π2px)^2 (π2py)^2 (π*2px)^2 (π*2py)^1.\n    Nb = 10, Na = 7.\n    Bond Order = 1/2 (10 - 7) = 1.5.\n\n4.  O2^2-: Total electrons = 16 + 2 = 18.\n    Electron configuration: (σ1s)^2 (σ*1s)^2 (σ2s)^2 (σ*2s)^2 (σ2pz)^2 (π2px)^2 (π2py)^2 (π*2px)^2 (π*2py)^2.\n    Nb = 10, Na = 8.\n    Bond Order = 1/2 (10 - 8) = 1.\n\nComparing the bond orders: O2+ (2.5) > O2 (2) > O2- (1.5) > O2^2- (1). Thus, O2+ has the highest bond order.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
