    "option_d": "CH3Cl",
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "Dipole moment arises from the unequal sharing of electrons between atoms in a molecule, creating partial positive and negative charges. A molecule has a zero dipole moment if its individual bond dipoles cancel out due to its symmetrical geometry.\n\n1.  H2O: V-shaped geometry. The two O-H bond dipoles do not cancel each other, resulting in a net dipole moment.\n2.  NH3: Trigonal pyramidal geometry. The N-H bond dipoles and the dipole due to the lone pair add up, resulting in a net dipole moment.\n3.  BF3: Trigonal planar geometry. The three B-F bond dipoles are equal in magnitude and are oriented at 120° to each other. Due to this perfect symmetry, they cancel each other out, resulting in a zero net dipole moment.\n4.  CH3Cl: Tetrahedral geometry with one Cl substituting one H. The C-Cl bond is highly polar, and the overall geometry is asymmetric, leading to a net dipole moment.\n\nTherefore, BF3 has a zero dipole moment.",
