    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "Hydrogen bonding is an electrostatic attraction between a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to a highly electronegative atom (like N, O, F) and another electronegative atom in the same or different molecule.\n\n1.  Intramolecular hydrogen bonding occurs when hydrogen bonding takes place within the same molecule.\n2.  Intermolecular hydrogen bonding occurs between different molecules.\n\n-   Water (H2O) and Ethanol (C2H5OH) both exhibit intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n-   p-Nitrophenol has the -OH and -NO2 groups far apart on the benzene ring (para position), so it predominantly forms intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n-   o-Nitrophenol has the -OH and -NO2 groups adjacent to each other (ortho position). The hydrogen atom of the hydroxyl group can form a hydrogen bond with one of the oxygen atoms of the nitro group within the same molecule. This is a classic example of intramolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nTherefore, o-Nitrophenol exhibits intramolecular hydrogen bonding.",
