  {
    "question": "According to Fajan's rules, which of the following halides has the maximum covalent character?",
    "option_a": "LiCl",
    "option_b": "NaCl",
    "option_c": "KCl",
    "option_d": "RbCl",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Fajan's rules help predict the degree of covalent character in an ionic bond. According to Fajan's rules:\n1.  Smaller size of the cation: A smaller cation has a higher charge density and thus a greater polarizing power, leading to increased covalent character.\n2.  Larger size of the anion: A larger anion is more easily distorted (polarized) by the cation, leading to increased covalent character.\n3.  Higher charge on cation/anion: Higher charge leads to greater polarizing power/polarizability, increasing covalent character.\n\nIn the given options (LiCl, NaCl, KCl, RbCl), the anion (Cl-) is the same. We need to compare the cations:\nLi+ < Na+ < K+ < Rb+ (increasing atomic size/ionic radius).\n\nSince Li+ is the smallest cation among the group 1 elements, it has the highest polarizing power. This means LiCl will have the greatest covalent character among the given compounds. As we move down the group from Li+ to Rb+, the cation size increases, polarizing power decreases, and the ionic character increases.",
