    "option_b": "2",
    "option_c": "3",
    "option_d": "4",
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "Resonance occurs when multiple Lewis structures can be drawn for a molecule or ion that differ only in the position of electrons (not atoms), and all contribute to the overall structure.\n\nFor the carbonate ion (CO3^2-):\n1.  The central atom is carbon. There are three oxygen atoms and a -2 charge.\n2.  A valid Lewis structure involves the carbon atom forming a double bond with one oxygen atom and single bonds with the other two oxygen atoms, each carrying a negative charge.\n3.  Because all three oxygen atoms are equivalent, the double bond can be delocalized over any of the three C-O positions.\n\nThis leads to three equivalent resonance structures:\n-   Structure 1: C=O with O1, C-O with O2, C-O with O3 (O2 and O3 carry -1 charge).\n-   Structure 2: C=O with O2, C-O with O1, C-O with O3 (O1 and O3 carry -1 charge).\n-   Structure 3: C=O with O3, C-O with O1, C-O with O2 (O1 and O2 carry -1 charge).\n\nThe actual structure is a resonance hybrid of these three structures, with partial double bond character on all C-O bonds and partial negative charges on all oxygen atoms.",
