    "solution": "All three molecules (CH4, NH3, H2O) have sp3 hybridization on their central atoms (C, N, O respectively). The ideal bond angle for sp3 hybridization is 109.5° (tetrahedral geometry).\n\nHowever, the presence of lone pairs on the central atom affects the bond angles due to increased repulsion.\n\n1.  CH4 (Methane): Central atom C has 4 bond pairs and 0 lone pairs. The geometry is perfectly tetrahedral, and the bond angle is 109.5°.\n2.  NH3 (Ammonia): Central atom N has 3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair. The lone pair-bond pair repulsion is greater than bond pair-bond pair repulsion. This compresses the H-N-H bond angles from 109.5° to approximately 107° (trigonal pyramidal geometry).\n3.  H2O (Water): Central atom O has 2 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs. The lone pair-lone pair repulsion is even stronger than lone pair-bond pair repulsion, and both are stronger than bond pair-bond pair repulsion. This causes an even greater compression of the H-O-H bond angle, reducing it from 109.5° to approximately 104.5° (bent or V-shaped geometry).\n\nTherefore, the correct order of bond angles is CH4 (109.5°) > NH3 (107°) > H2O (104.5°).",
