    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Lattice energy is the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into gaseous constituent ions. It is directly proportional to the product of the charges of the ions and inversely proportional to the sum of their radii (interionic distance).\n\nLattice Energy ∝ (q1 * q2) / (r1 + r2)\n\nIn the given compounds (LiCl, NaCl, KCl, RbCl), the anion (Cl-) has a constant charge and size. The cations are Li+, Na+, K+, and Rb+. All have a +1 charge.\n\nThe order of increasing ionic radii for these cations is:\nLi+ < Na+ < K+ < Rb+\n\nAs the size of the cation increases, the interionic distance (r1 + r2) increases. Since lattice energy is inversely proportional to the interionic distance, a smaller interionic distance leads to higher lattice energy.\n\nTherefore, LiCl, having the smallest cation (Li+), will have the smallest interionic distance and thus the highest lattice energy. The lattice energy decreases as the cation size increases.\n\nCorrect order: LiCl > NaCl > KCl > RbCl.",
