    "option_d": "CO2",
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "The octet rule states that atoms tend to gain, lose, or share electrons until they are surrounded by eight valence electrons.\n\n1.  H2O: Oxygen has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs, total 8 electrons. Obeys octet rule.\n2.  NH3: Nitrogen has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair, total 8 electrons. Obeys octet rule.\n3.  PCl5: Phosphorus is the central atom. It forms 5 single bonds with 5 chlorine atoms. Therefore, Phosphorus is surrounded by 5 x 2 = 10 valence electrons. This is an example of an expanded octet, where the central atom has more than 8 valence electrons. Thus, PCl5 does not obey the octet rule.\n4.  CO2: Carbon forms two double bonds with two oxygen atoms. Each double bond contributes 4 electrons to the central carbon. So, carbon has 2 x 4 = 8 electrons. Obeys octet rule.\n\nTherefore, PCl5 does not obey the octet rule.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "topic": "Exceptions to Octet Rule"
