    "correct_answer": "C",
    "solution": "Bond strength generally increases with increasing bond order (the number of bonds between two atoms). A higher bond order means more electrons are shared between the atoms, leading to a stronger attraction between the nuclei and thus a stronger bond.\n\n1.  C-C: Single bond, bond order = 1.\n2.  C=C: Double bond, bond order = 2.\n3.  C≡C: Triple bond, bond order = 3.\n4.  C-H: Single bond, bond order = 1.\n\nComparing bond orders, the C≡C triple bond has the highest bond order (3), followed by C=C (2), and then C-C and C-H (both 1). While C-H bonds are generally strong, triple bonds are typically stronger than any single or double bonds involving the same atoms. The bond energy values reflect this: C≡C (~839 kJ/mol) > C=C (~614 kJ/mol) > C-H (~413 kJ/mol) > C-C (~348 kJ/mol).\n\nTherefore, the C≡C bond is the strongest among the given options.",
