    "question": "Which of the following compounds exhibits back bonding (pπ-pπ back bonding)?",
    "option_a": "BF3",
    "option_b": "CCl4",
    "option_c": "SiH4",
    "option_d": "NH3",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "Back bonding (or pπ-pπ back bonding) occurs when a lone pair of electrons from a more electronegative atom (typically a halogen, oxygen, or nitrogen) is donated into an adjacent empty p-orbital of another atom (typically from Group 13 elements like B or Al, or elements from Period 3 onwards like Si, P, S having empty d-orbitals but also empty p-orbitals in some cases, often forming pπ-dπ bonds).\n\n1.  BF3: Boron (Group 13) has an incomplete octet in BF3 (6 valence electrons). It has an empty 2p orbital. Fluorine atoms have lone pairs in their 2p orbitals. One of the lone pairs from a fluorine atom can be delocalized into the empty 2p orbital of boron, forming a partial double bond. This is a classic example of pπ-pπ back bonding, increasing the stability of BF3 and making B-F bonds shorter and stronger than expected for a single bond.\n2.  CCl4: Carbon does not have any empty p-orbitals to accept electron pairs. Chlorine has lone pairs but carbon cannot accommodate them.\n3.  SiH4: Silicon (Group 14) has empty d-orbitals, but hydrogen does not have lone pairs to donate.\n4.  NH3: Nitrogen has a lone pair, but hydrogen does not have empty orbitals to accept them.\n\nTherefore, BF3 exhibits back bonding.",
