    "question": "Arrange the following acids in increasing order of their acidic strength: HF, HCl, HBr, HI.",
    "option_a": "HF < HCl < HBr < HI",
    "option_b": "HI < HBr < HCl < HF",
    "option_c": "HCl < HBr < HF < HI",
    "option_d": "HBr < HCl < HI < HF",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution": "The acidic strength of hydrogen halides (HX) in aqueous solution depends on the ease with which they can donate a proton (H+). This ease is primarily governed by the bond dissociation energy of the H-X bond and the stability of the conjugate base (X-).\n\nAs we move down Group 17 (F to I):\n\n1.  **Atomic Size:** The atomic size of the halogen increases (F < Cl < Br < I).\n2.  **Bond Length:** Consequently, the H-X bond length increases (H-F < H-Cl < H-Br < H-I).\n3.  **Bond Dissociation Energy:** As the bond length increases, the H-X bond becomes weaker and easier to break. Therefore, the bond dissociation energy decreases down the group.\n    (H-F > H-Cl > H-Br > H-I)\n4.  **Acidic Strength:** A weaker H-X bond means it is easier for the acid to lose a proton, leading to increased acidic strength.\n    (HF < HCl < HBr < HI)\n\nAlthough fluorine is the most electronegative, which would make the H-F bond highly polar, the very strong H-F bond (due to its short length) makes it the least acidic among the hydrogen halides in aqueous solution. The stability of the conjugate base also plays a role, with larger anions (like I-) better able to delocalize the negative charge, making them more stable. However, bond strength is the dominant factor here.\n\nTherefore, the increasing order of acidic strength is HF < HCl < HBr < HI.",
