    "question": "Which of the following compounds has the highest percentage of ionic character?",
    "option_a": "H2O",
    "option_b": "HCl",
    "option_c": "HBr",
    "option_d": "HF",
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "solution": "The percentage of ionic character in a covalent bond is directly related to the electronegativity difference between the two bonded atoms. A larger electronegativity difference leads to a more polar bond and thus a higher percentage of ionic character.\n\nLet's consider the electronegativity values of the atoms involved (Pauling scale):\n-   Hydrogen (H): ~2.20\n-   Oxygen (O): ~3.44\n-   Fluorine (F): ~3.98\n-   Chlorine (Cl): ~3.16\n-   Bromine (Br): ~2.96\n\nNow, let's calculate the electronegativity difference (ΔEN) for each bond:\n-   H2O (O-H bond): ΔEN = 3.44 - 2.20 = 1.24\n-   HCl (Cl-H bond): ΔEN = 3.16 - 2.20 = 0.96\n-   HBr (Br-H bond): ΔEN = 2.96 - 2.20 = 0.76\n-   HF (F-H bond): ΔEN = 3.98 - 2.20 = 1.78\n\nComparing the electronegativity differences:\nHF (1.78) > H2O (1.24) > HCl (0.96) > HBr (0.76)\n\nSince HF has the largest electronegativity difference, the H-F bond is the most polar and thus has the highest percentage of ionic character among the given compounds.\n\n(Note: While H2O has two O-H bonds, we compare the polarity of individual bonds to determine ionic character. The O-H bond in H2O is less polar than the H-F bond in HF).",
